 fig.suptitle('Do recorded minutes improve the deletion baseline? | 24 qualified teams');fig.tight_layout();fig.savefig(out/'minutes_model_comparison.pdf');fig.savefig(out/'minutes_model_comparison.png',dpi=150);plt.close(fig)
 text=['# Steps 1–3: deletion baseline, minutes diagnostic and common players','The original prior-season on/off-selected players and existing trade-safe windows are retained. All 30 teams are illustrated; primary comparisons use the same 24 teams with ≥15 played and ≥10 missed games. No new NBA requests are required.','## Step 1 — baseline','D is the observed star-present passing matrix with the star row/column zeroed. No replacement passes are invented. Graphs and comparisons normalize counts to directed edge shares.','## Step 2 — how minutes are distributed','No equal split or manual allocation of the star’s minutes is made. For each teammate, sum their recorded minutes in the actual missed games. Those are the diagnostic exposure inputs. The minute table also reports total minutes divided by the number of team games in each condition, so nonappearances are included as zero exposure. These averages are not minutes per appearance.','For a sender i with baseline minutes >0, rate_i = nonstar outgoing passes in played games / recorded played-game minutes. Expected missed-game outgoing passes = rate_i × their recorded total minutes in missed games. For each sender, divide these expected passes among their usual nonstar recipients in baseline proportions. Recipients with zero missed-game minutes are removed and the remaining probabilities renormalized. Self-passes and star passes remain zero. No actual missed-game passing edge is used to set these rates or preferences.','A sender with no baseline minutes has no estimable player-specific rate: use the pooled baseline nonstar outgoing-passes-per-minute rate. If no baseline recipient support is available, allocate their expected passes among available teammates in proportion to recorded missed-game minutes. This explicit fallback is an assumption, not learned behavior; every affected sender and predicted pass share is exported. A previously observed player with zero passes and positive baseline minutes retains rate zero. Recorded minutes can be rounded; unsupported rates are flagged through fallback metadata.','The additional both-endpoints sensitivity multiplies each baseline recipient weight by that recipient’s missed/played minute-total ratio, then normalizes the sender row back to the same predicted outgoing total. A global ratio of game counts cancels within a row. This models recipient exposure approximately; it is not joint lineup/co-presence data. It changes conditional choices mechanically through exposure and is not the primary unchanged-preference model.','The gains in teammates’ minutes do not have to sum to the star’s minutes: other players also gain or lose time, and overtime and recorded-minute rounding differ between samples. The exported positive and negative minute changes reconcile with star removal and the change in total recorded team minutes. Do not attribute every teammate’s gain to the star.','## Step 3 — same-player comparison','Restrict to nonstar players with positive passing involvement and positive recorded minutes in both contexts. Extract exactly the same sender/recipient identities from deletion and actual networks. Rebuild the minutes predictions within that restricted matrix using the same recorded minutes. This isolates roster entry/exit from the comparison, but not shared-court time, opponents or other injuries.','Compare directed-edge total variation (0=identical normalized pass shares, 1=disjoint), involvement-share distance, and conditional recipient Jensen–Shannon divergence. For conditional JS, use the same actual sender weights across models. Sender-only reweighting preserves each common sender’s recipient probabilities by construction; its conditional divergence should remain the same. Recipient-exposure sensitivity can change that divergence. Residual differences are unexplained network changes, not proven tactical adaptation.','## Summary','|Sample|n|Median baseline TV|Median sender-minutes TV|Median both-endpoints TV|Sender improved|Common-panel sender improved|','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
 for z in s.itertuples():text.append(f'|{z.sample}|{z.n}|{z.median_deletion_edge_tv:.3f}|{z.median_sender_minutes_edge_tv:.3f}|{z.median_both_endpoints_edge_tv:.3f}|{z.sender_improved_teams}/{z.n}|{z.common_sender_improved_teams}/{z.n}|')
 text+=['## Limits','Recorded missed-game minutes are post-absence information: this is an in-sample diagnostic, not a deployable forecast or out-of-sample validation. Total minutes do not establish which players shared court time. Baseline played games pool dates before and after absences. Passes-per-minute estimates can be unstable for rarely used players. New-player fallback support may be poor. Improved distance is not a percentage of causally explained change. A useful next model must estimate minutes from strictly earlier games and test on later absences.']
